<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Jennings</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1715">1715</a>–<a href="/years/1720">1720</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Jennings was an English privateer and pirate based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>. In 1716 he raided the Spanish salvage camps of the wrecked 1715 treasure fleet off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, and he became one of the leading captains of the pirate community at Nassau. He accepted the royal pardon offered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodes-rogers">Woodes Rogers</a> in 1718, helping to end the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">Pirates</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jennings came from a family well established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermuda">Bermuda</a>, a colony closely tied to privateering, and called himself a Bermudian. The author Colin Woodard describes him as an educated ship captain with a comfortable estate, and he held property in both Bermuda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. He is first recorded as a privateer operating from Jamaica during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, when the island was governed by Lord Archibald Hamilton.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 31 July 1715 all 11 ships of the Spanish treasure fleet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> were wrecked by a hurricane on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> coast near Cape Canaveral. Hamilton commissioned Jennings's sloop Bersheba and John Wills's Eagle to act against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> only, though it was later claimed that he had invested in the ships and backed an attack on the wrecks, which he denied. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vane">Charles Vane</a>, Jennings learned the position of the main salvage camp from a captured Spanish mail ship, and his men drove off about 60 soldiers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> de Ayz, taking some £87,500 in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><p>Jennings returned to the wrecks more than once, accepting 25,000 pieces of eight from the salvagers to leave them alone and then plundering them anyway. In April 1716 his fleet captured the French merchant ship St. Marie off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, but Samuel Bellamy and Paulsgrave Williams seized it from his prize crew and escaped with 28,500 pieces of eight. In revenge Jennings destroyed one of Bellamy's boats, presumably with men aboard, and burned an English merchant sloop; more than 20 Frenchmen and Englishmen were killed.</p><p>Under Spanish pressure Hamilton forbade commissioned ships to raid the wrecks, and Jennings, now treated as a pirate, made Nassau on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-providence">New Providence</a> his base. Its pirate population grew from dozens to hundreds, and he and his bitter rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-hornigold">Benjamin Hornigold</a> dominated what became known as the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">Pirates</a>. In November 1716 Jennings captured Hamilton's own ship, the Hamilton Galley, as the deposed governor sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodes-rogers">Woodes Rogers</a>, the new Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, offered a royal pardon to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> who surrendered, and early in 1718 Jennings gave himself up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermuda">Bermuda</a>, one of 400 who took the amnesty. He later sailed as a privateer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-quadruple-alliance">War of the Quadruple Alliance</a>. His end is unknown: some sources have him retiring as a wealthy planter in Bermuda, while some historians suggest he was captured by the Spanish or French in a later war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry Jennings” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Benjamin Hornigold and <b>Henry Jennings</b> were leading figures, alongside Edward Teach, Charles Vane, Sam Bellamy and Calico Jack Rackham.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-pirates">Republic of Pirates</a></li></ul></section>
